Ladybird gives every extension context i18n.getMessage() and
getUILanguage(), both answered from the extension’s descriptor
without IPC. Chromium’s GetI18nMessage() reads its SharedL10nMap,
and HandleGetUILanguage() returns base::i18n::GetConfiguredLocale();
Gecko’s ext-i18n.js uses LocaleData.localizeMessage() and uiLocale,
and WebKit’s WebExtensionAPILocalization::getMessage() reads the
context’s localization data.

A message name that isn’t a string, or options that aren’t an
object, make getMessage() throw a TypeError, as Chromium’s APISignature
checks do. Gecko’s schema checks throw too, but a plain Error.
Past those checks, Ladybird follows GetI18nMessage(): An array of more
than nine substitutions returns undefined, an element whose conversion
throws is skipped, a getter that throws returns undefined, and escapeLt
applies before substitution.

Ladybird localizes a text/css response after ResourceLoader::
handle_extension_load_request() reads its bytes, so a sheet pulled in
by @import is localized the same way, exactly once. Chromium does it in
ExtensionLocalizationThrottle, through SharedL10nMap::ReplaceMessages();
Gecko’s ExtensionProtocolHandler::SubstituteChannel() runs
its CSS through a localization stream converter; and WebKit’s
WebExtensionContext::localizedResourceString() handles served
CSS as well as injected CSS.
